:\tsynctest.Test(t, func(t *testing.T) {\n229:func TestIntervalChangeAppliesOnNextWait(t *testing.T) {\n230:\tsynctest.Test(t, func(t *testing.T) {\n257:func TestTickTimeoutOverride(t *testing.T) {\n258:\tsynctest.Test(t, func(t *testing.T) {\n290:func TestWakeRunsTickNow(t *testing.T) {\n291:\tsynctest.Test(t, func(t *testing.T) {\n309:func TestWakeRespectsKillSwitch(t *testing.T) {\n310:\tsynctest.Test(t, func(t *testing.T) {\n329:func TestWakeDuringTickCoalesces(t *testing.T) {\n330:\tsynctest.Test(t, func(t *testing.T) {\n353:func TestWakeAfterStopIgnored(t *testing.T) {\ndocs/proposal.md:303:**One pool of job slots, drained from the end.** At most `max_pods` jobs (default 3) run at once, shared by every loop. When a slot frees, the controller fills it in this order: reviewers, then workers, then triage, then scanners, oldest first within each. Finishing work in flight beats starting new work, so a busy day grows the scanner backlog, not the pile of half-done PRs. The merger and the Reaper are controller code and take no slot. Slots are granted when a loop polls and nothing is queued: a loop that found work waiting within the last two of its intervals has that demand reserved ahead of lower classes, so a freed slot can sit idle until that loop's next poll. A scanner run that gets no slot is skipped, not queued. The pool also starts no agent job for an exhausted account or model family until its reset: a job that stops at a subscription usage limit records the limit (in the controller database, so a restart keeps it), and the pool answers `limited` to any candidate whose role runs on that account or family (a whole-account session or weekly limit blocks every role; an Opus or Sonnet limit blocks only roles on that family, and a role with no model, which runs the CLI default, is blocked by any limit). A limited candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand, so lower classes are not held back. The block ends at the reset time the CLI reported; when it reported none the first pause is 30 minutes and each further stop with no reset doubles it up to 4 hours, starting over once a job succeeds; a reset time further away than 8 days is clamped. Running jobs are never killed for a limit. Deterministic scanners and the merger use no model and are unaffected. A daily dollar cap works the same way at the same gate: the pool answers `budget` to a candidate whose role cap or the repo cap is reached, and that candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand. A job that already holds a slot keeps it and finishes.\ndocs/proposal.md:366:2. **Pin the head SHA.** Before claiming, the loop fetches the branch into the controller's clone and requires the tip to equal the PR's head SHA; on a mismatch it retries on the next poll. The match also shows the head is this repository's branch, so a fork PR with a colliding branch name is never reviewed. The job's worktree is cut at that commit, so the review describes exactly one commit even if the branch moves.\ndocs/proposal.md:411:- the diff touches no protected path; the list comes only from `merge.protected_paths`, with no floor in code (decided, #3), so a human who empties the list removes the gate. `ard init` proposes the defaults `.github/`, `.ard/config.yaml`, the four role trees (`.ard/scanners/`, `.ard/triage/`, `.ard/workers/`, `.ard/reviewers/`), the three repo context docs (`.ard/PRODUCT.md`, `.ard/VISION.md`, `.ard/TECH_STACK.md`, named one by one, with no `.ard/*.md` glob), `**/CODEOWNERS`, and the agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents: `CLAUDE.md`, `**/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `**/AGENTS.md`, `.claude/` and `.mcp.json`, and the linter config a PR could weaken, `**/.golangci.yml`, `**/.golangci.yaml`, `**/.golangci.toml` and `**/.golangci.json` (20 entries; `**/X` already matches a root `X`, the bare entries are kept for clarity). An entry ending in `/` is a directory prefix; any other entry is a doublestar glob. Matching is case-insensitive on the repo-relative path (macOS file systems are, so `Claude.md` and `.Claude/x` are protected). A prefix matches on a directory boundary (`.github/` matches `.github` and `.github/x`, never `.githubx`). A glob follows doublestar: `*` stays within one path segment and `**` crosses them, so a bare `CODEOWNERS` would match only the root file. Quote globs that start with `*` in YAML. The merger checks both names of a renamed file; a malformed path counts as protected. A PR that touches one gets `ard:needs-approval` and merges on the next poll once a person with write access approves the current head with a GitHub review (below).\ndocs/proposal.md:533:- **Kill switch.** Four sources stop new claims, scanner runs (filing and autoclose), stale closes and merges: a local pause file `~/.ard/pause/<owner>/<repo>` outside the repository (the path is lowercased; `ard serve` creates the directory, so `touch` pauses and `rm` resumes), `paused: true` in the `config.yaml` in the host's working tree, `ard:pause` on the pinned status issue, and `paused: true` in the config on `origin/main`. They are checked cheapest first, before every claiming tick. The delay is at most one loop interval plus 10% jitter; a tick already running may finish its claims. `SIGUSR1` (Unix only) makes every loop run one tick now, logged as `controller.wake`; the tick still passes the kill switch and the job pool, and wakes that arrive while a tick runs coalesce into one extra tick. The gate answers one controller-wide question and has no per-kind checks, so every loop that writes (scanner filing and autoclose, stale closes, merges, claims), except the Reaper, the cost-table writer and in-flight close-outs, must report `Claims() == true` and is skipped while paused. Running jobs, in-flight close-outs, the Reaper and the `costs` loop continue: the table is bookkeeping about work already done, not new work. The check fails closed: an unreadable status issue, a failed fetch of `origin/main`, or a config that fails to load counts as paused. The pause file pauses when anything exists at the path (a file, a directory or a symlink; content is ignored), and a path that cannot be examined, for example because `pause/<owner>` is a regular file, also counts as paused. The local working-tree `config.yaml` is read only for its `paused` key; a missing, unreadable or invalid file does not pause by itself, but a readable one that says `paused: true` does. The pause file and the local `paused` are the kill switches that nothing pushed to GitHub can lift. Config and role directories otherwise come from `origin/main` at the same SHA the tick's jobs use (see Configuration). `ard serve` remembers the status issue number in `_controller.db` (`meta` key `status_issue`). At startup it reads that issue directly and adopts it if it is still open, titled `ard status`, carries the `<!-- ard:status -->` marker and has the App as author; otherwise it searches by those same tests, and creates and pins the issue if none matches. Search is eventually consistent, so the remembered number is what stops a quick restart from creating a second issue; if two still match, the lowest number wins. A cached issue that cannot be read for a reason other than not-found falls back to search rather than aborting startup. A dry-run never stores its synthetic (negative) number; a real number found by search is stored only in the separate dry-run database. A second `ard serve` on the same repo exits with an error: the leader lock is an `flock` on `~/.ard/locks/<owner>/<repo>.lock` (local filesystem only), shared by live and dry-run.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5ff4ebf2-2666-4434-877a-923cd6f2f050","uuid":"f6da502f-fbe6-4719-adc8-16cba79cf200","timestamp":"2026-11-25T12:12:22.887Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"go: downloading gopkg.in/yaml.v3 v3.0.1\ngo: downloading github.com/bradleyfalzon/ghinstallation/v2 v2.19.0\ngo: downloading github.com/google/go-github/v88 v88.0.0\ngo: downloading golang.org/x/time v0.16.